.TH "ifstat" 1G "5 October 2026" GINI "gRouter Commands"

.SH NAME
ifstat \- traffic counters for each interface

.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
ifstat
.fi

.SH DESCRIPTION

Prints, for every interface, how many bytes and packets it has received and sent since the
router started. One line per interface:
.RS
.nf
IFSTAT id device ip rx_bytes rx_pkts tx_bytes tx_pkts
.fi
.RE

The counters only ever grow. To get bandwidth, take two readings some seconds apart: the
difference in bytes, times 8, divided by the seconds between them, is the rate in bits per
second. The Router Lab's bandwidth meter does exactly this every second or so.

Every frame counts, including ARP and the router's own packets, so an idle link still shows a
little traffic.

.SH EXAMPLES

.RS
.nf
ifstat
IFSTAT 1 tun1 10.0.1.1 5124 52 4998 51
IFSTAT 2 tun2 10.0.2.1 4998 51 5124 52
.fi
.RE

Here tun1 has received 5124 bytes in 52 packets. The two interfaces mirror each other because
everything that came in one went out the other.

.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR watch (1G),
.BR ifconfig (1G)
